<h2>The scheme and the trail</h2>
<p>The complainant was drawn into an investment scheme run through a WhatsApp persona and cheated of Rs 36.40 lakh. As is now standard in cyber fraud, the proceeds did not sit in one account: they were layered through several. One co-accused lent his bank account for Rs 7.5 lakh of the funds; another allegedly sent Rs 90,000 to the petitioner, who transferred Rs 51,000 onward.</p>
<p>The petitioner — a 47-year-old from Punjab with two minor children, no antecedents, and a small informal money-lending business — explained the Rs 90,000 as an ordinary loan transaction and attacked the case as resting on uncorroborated co-accused disclosures. The State pressed her evasive and inconsistent answers about the money, the non-recovery of cheque books, banking kits and ATM cards, and a money trail still untraced.</p>

<h2>Why custodial interrogation won</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. The trail runs through people.</strong> Layered transactions cannot be reconstructed from statements recorded at liberty; the Court accepted that custodial interrogation may be necessary to map the flow and identify other beneficiaries.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Documents, not just disclosures.</strong> The allegations rested on banking records — objective material — and not solely on what co-accused said, distinguishing the cases where disclosure statements alone were held insufficient.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Inconsistency as corroboration of need.</strong> The petitioner&rsquo;s shifting explanations did not prove guilt, but they demonstrated exactly why unsupervised questioning had failed and custodial questioning was sought.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. The economic-offences lens.</strong> Applying <em>Srikant Upadhyay</em>, <em>P. Krishna Mohan Reddy</em> and <em>P. Chidambaram v. Directorate of Enforcement</em>, the Court treated financial crime as a class warranting a stricter approach to pre-arrest protection.</div>
</div>

<h2>The counter-current: when such pleas succeed</h2>
<p>The same bench, on the same day, granted anticipatory bail in an NDPS matter where the only links were call records and bank transfers explained as personal loans — because there the material rested on a co-accused&rsquo;s disclosure without substantive corroboration. Read together, the two orders draw the working line: bare transfers plus disclosure statements, plausibly explained, may support protection; an untraced layered trail plus inconsistent explanations will not. The difference is not the amount involved but the state of the objective record.</p>
<div class="note">For account holders approached to &ldquo;receive and pass on&rdquo; funds — often for a commission — the criminal exposure is real even for small sums. Delhi&rsquo;s cyber fraud prosecutions routinely reach every account in the chain, and the mule-account defence of an innocent loan depends entirely on consistent, documented explanations given at the first opportunity.</div>

<h2>Practical guidance for applicants</h2>
<p>Three lessons follow for anyone whose account has received tainted funds. First, reconstruct the transaction documentarily before any appearance: loan agreements, repayment history, contemporaneous messages. Second, answer the investigating officer consistently; the fastest route to a custodial-interrogation finding is a story that changes between appearances. Third, where the material genuinely begins and ends with a co-accused&rsquo;s statement, say so with precision — that remains the strongest ground on which pre-arrest protection is granted in this class of cases.</p>
